WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.270
Film Courage: We had a few questions come
in from our Youtube viewers and this one is

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:08.790
from Ms. Green and she writes ‘How have
what you’ve seen over time “change”

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:11.790
your view of life and does it ever show up
in your filming?

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:13.860
Did you edit it out?

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:19.900
Alexandria Bombach, Documentary Filmmaker:
I think the films I’m making are definitely

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:30.220
starting from my experience and a lot of them
and how I’m questioning how we are operating

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.480
in this human experience at all.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:38.329
So with FRAME BY FRAME there were just so
many questions I was having as a storyteller

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:43.030
about our perception and where that comes
from.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:48.829
I wanted to make a film about Afghan photographers
to talk about perception of a place that is

00:00:48.829 --> 00:00:54.400
so often this unknown known in a lot of ways.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:00.770
And with ON HER SHOULDERS I was really interested
and this was kind of feeding off of that in

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:06.670
a way where it was continuing telling a story
about storytelling and the repetition and

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:12.920
the packaging of stories of trauma and how
we’re receiving that as an audience and

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:18.439
our own relationship with apathy and empathy
through these stories.

00:01:18.439 --> 00:01:25.049
And so I don’t think I would make a film
where I had already had the answer.

00:01:25.049 --> 00:01:32.500
I already feel very strongly about cigarettes
and single use plastics and I don’t think

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:36.479
I’d ever make a film about it because I
already know how I feel about it.

00:01:36.479 --> 00:01:43.439
I think all of these films are already questions
that I’m already asking myself and I think

00:01:43.439 --> 00:01:50.779
that’s where that energy comes from and
relates to an audience asking themselves questions,

00:01:50.779 --> 00:01:51.779
too.

00:01:51.779 --> 00:01:58.409
I think that is where is comes from is my
own questioning of life and how I’m experiencing

00:01:58.409 --> 00:01:59.770
the world.

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:03.049
Film Courage: Where does your love for storytelling
come from?

00:02:03.049 --> 00:02:09.269
You had gone to study marketing and I can
see why you took your love of the environment

00:02:09.269 --> 00:02:13.810
and things and wanted to add that to you marketing
plan.

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:15.590
Alexandria: Maybe?

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:21.790
Film Courage: But where does your love for
storytelling and film come from?

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:31.150
Alexandria: I’ve definitely have loved making
video since I was a kid.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:36.930
I don’t know it’s a hard question to ask
where does my love for storytelling come from

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:42.439
because I’m having such a complicated relationship
with storytelling even now with ON HER SHOULDERS

00:02:42.439 --> 00:02:46.299
and really questioning where we are coming
from with storytellers and our responsibility

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:51.939
to our subjects and who is telling what story
and why we are telling it and all sorts of

00:02:51.939 --> 00:02:54.629
things and so it’s an interesting space
to be in now.

00:02:54.629 --> 00:03:00.409
I know that I don’t want to stop this work
and will continue to make documentaries and

00:03:00.409 --> 00:03:06.250
probably die with a camera in my hands but
I don’t even know where that comes from.

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:08.359
I have no idea.

00:03:08.359 --> 00:03:15.609
I feel like each film that I make is kind
of an exploration of things that I’m questioning

00:03:15.609 --> 00:03:16.609
about life.

00:03:16.609 --> 00:03:22.420
It’s a process of pulling things out of
myself and answering hard questions.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:33.170
So I just love the process of it (I love all
of that) and of course it introduces me to

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:43.120
people I would have never had…it’s such
an honor to have met Nadia even though I wish

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:45.230
of course I would have never met Nadia.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:51.590
I wish none of this would have ever happened
and that she was just an unknown person in

00:03:51.590 --> 00:04:02.319
a village in Sinjar but I am so, so thankful
that I got to meet these people and be exposed

00:04:02.319 --> 00:04:10.459
to their patience and grace and empathy so
I can see the world in a different way.

00:04:10.459 --> 00:04:14.739
It’s quite a gift and hopefully that’s
translated to the audience too.

